WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.849
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level two.

2
00:00:06.149 --> 00:00:09.513
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.813 --> 00:00:15.623
Unit twelve: Supply Chain Management. Today you will speculate about the past with the third conditional.

4
00:00:15.923 --> 00:00:18.726
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.667 --> 00:00:23.364
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.217 --> 00:00:25.671
Logistics network.

7
00:00:26.683 --> 00:00:31.167
Logistics network means the system of transport and storage that moves goods.

8
00:00:31.783 --> 00:00:34.596
The logistics network connects farms to cities.

9
00:00:35.183 --> 00:00:38.555
Your turn. Say: Logistics network.

10
00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:42.970
Logistics network.

11
00:00:43.717 --> 00:00:45.287
Inventory control.

12
00:00:46.283 --> 00:00:50.094
Inventory control means managing how much stock a company keeps.

13
00:00:50.683 --> 00:00:52.973
Inventory control reduces waste.

14
00:00:53.583 --> 00:00:56.816
Your turn. Say: Inventory control.

15
00:00:59.667 --> 00:01:01.469
Inventory control.

16
00:01:02.217 --> 00:01:03.856
Supplier relationship.

17
00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:09.915
Supplier relationship means the ongoing business connection with a company that provides goods.

18
00:01:10.517 --> 00:01:13.457
A strong supplier relationship prevents delays.

19
00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:17.503
Your turn. Say: Supplier relationship.

20
00:01:20.367 --> 00:01:22.088
Supplier relationship.

21
00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:24.362
Just-in-time delivery.

22
00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:29.149
Just-in-time delivery means receiving goods only when they are needed.

23
00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:32.585
Just-in-time delivery saves storage costs.

24
00:01:33.183 --> 00:01:36.985
Your turn. Say: Just-in-time delivery.

25
00:01:39.833 --> 00:01:41.543
Just-in-time delivery.

26
00:01:42.283 --> 00:01:43.818
Distribution center.

27
00:01:44.817 --> 00:01:48.883
Distribution center means a large building where goods are stored and sent out.

28
00:01:49.483 --> 00:01:52.702
The distribution center employs two hundred people.

29
00:01:53.317 --> 00:01:56.306
Your turn. Say: Distribution center.

30
00:01:59.167 --> 00:02:00.655
Distribution center.

31
00:02:01.417 --> 00:02:02.418
Bottleneck.

32
00:02:03.417 --> 00:02:06.217
Bottleneck means a point where progress is slowed down.

33
00:02:06.817 --> 00:02:08.596
The port became a bottleneck.

34
00:02:09.183 --> 00:02:11.662
Your turn. Say: Bottleneck.

35
00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:15.478
Bottleneck.

36
00:02:16.217 --> 00:02:17.427
Freight cost.

37
00:02:18.417 --> 00:02:21.333
Freight cost means the price of transporting goods.

38
00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:24.727
Higher freight costs raised shop prices.

39
00:02:25.317 --> 00:02:27.981
Your turn. Say: Freight cost.

40
00:02:30.833 --> 00:02:32.206
Freight cost.

41
00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:34.578
Supply disruption.

42
00:02:35.583 --> 00:02:39.092
Supply disruption means an interruption in the flow of goods.

43
00:02:39.683 --> 00:02:42.182
A supply disruption emptied the shelves.

44
00:02:42.783 --> 00:02:45.691
Your turn. Say: Supply disruption.

45
00:02:48.533 --> 00:02:49.941
Supply disruption.

46
00:02:50.733 --> 00:02:54.338
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:55.183 --> 00:03:01.040
Visal, a report says 'If the port had stayed open, the shelves would not have emptied.' What is that tense?

48
00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:10.201
The third conditional: if + past perfect, would have + past participle. It imagines a different past.

49
00:03:11.317 --> 00:03:13.792
Is it common in business analysis?

50
00:03:14.883 --> 00:03:24.330
Very. 'If the firm had held more inventory, the supply disruption would not have stopped production.' Analysts use it to learn lessons.

51
00:03:25.417 --> 00:03:26.987
What else could have been done?

52
00:03:28.083 --> 00:03:36.782
If managers had built stronger supplier relationships, the bottleneck might have been avoided, and freight costs would have stayed lower.

53
00:03:37.933 --> 00:03:39.457
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:40.317 --> 00:03:45.277
Grammar focus: Third conditional: if plus had plus past participle.

55
00:03:45.577 --> 00:03:46.752
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:47.450 --> 00:03:50.611
If the port had stayed open, shelves would not have emptied.

57
00:03:51.517 --> 00:03:54.851
If the firm had held more stock, production would have continued.

58
00:03:55.750 --> 00:03:59.549
If suppliers had warned them, managers might have avoided the bottleneck.

59
00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:09.364
Third conditional = if + had + past participle, would have + past participle. It talks about unreal past situations and regrets.

60
00:04:10.117 --> 00:04:13.442
Be careful! Don't say: If he knew, he would have help.

61
00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:18.212
Say: If he had known, he would have helped.

62
00:04:18.512 --> 00:04:21.487
Third conditional: had + past participle.

63
00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:25.126
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:25.933 --> 00:04:27.712
Lessons from a Disruption.

65
00:04:28.012 --> 00:04:32.589
When a major port closed, a global logistics network suffered a serious bottleneck.

66
00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:37.399
If managers had held more inventory, factories would not have stopped.

67
00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:44.579
Just-in-time delivery had saved money for years, yet it left no safety margin during the supply disruption.

68
00:04:45.567 --> 00:04:51.781
If suppliers and distribution centers had shared information earlier, freight costs might have stayed lower.

69
00:04:52.767 --> 00:04:55.858
Companies now balance efficiency with resilience.

70
00:04:56.967 --> 00:05:00.910
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:01.750 --> 00:05:04.748
If the port blank open, shelves would not have emptied.

72
00:05:05.048 --> 00:05:09.085
A, had stayed. B, stayed. C, would stay.

73
00:05:12.633 --> 00:05:14.772
The answer is A: had stayed.

74
00:05:15.917 --> 00:05:18.578
A point where progress is slowed down is a blank.

75
00:05:18.878 --> 00:05:22.357
A, bottleneck. B, freight. C, network.

76
00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:27.783
The answer is A: bottleneck.

77
00:05:28.950 --> 00:05:31.936
Receiving goods only when needed is blank delivery.

78
00:05:32.236 --> 00:05:35.994
A, bulk. B, just-in-time. C, inventory.

79
00:05:39.533 --> 00:05:42.067
The answer is B: just-in-time.

80
00:05:43.217 --> 00:05:44.914
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:45.214 --> 00:05:51.515
A, If he had known, he would have helped.. B, If he knew, he would have help.. C, If he had known, he would helped..

82
00:05:55.067 --> 00:05:57.821
The answer is A: If he had known, he would have helped..

83
00:05:58.983 --> 00:06:02.225
According to the reading, companies now balance efficiency with…

84
00:06:02.525 --> 00:06:06.585
A, resilience. B, luck. C, advertising.

85
00:06:10.133 --> 00:06:12.493
The answer is A: resilience.

86
00:06:13.700 --> 00:06:16.886
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:17.750 --> 00:06:21.805
How can a supply disruption in one country affect stores in another country?

88
00:06:30.617 --> 00:06:32.059
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:32.359 --> 00:06:57.586
Many products depend on parts or materials from several countries. If a factory or port stops working in one place, the next stage cannot begin, and goods arrive late or not at all. Stores in another country then have empty shelves or must raise prices because freight costs rise. This shows how connected the modern supply chain is.

90
00:06:57.886 --> 00:06:59.491
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:00.383 --> 00:07:06.867
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and third conditional: if plus had plus past participle.

92
00:07:07.167 --> 00:07:11.956
Today's value is systems thinking. See how the parts connect to make the whole.

93
00:07:12.256 --> 00:07:14.954
See you in Unit thirteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:15.254 --> 00:07:18.534
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

